 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "e2",
   "source_id": "exr:4.5.2",
   "source_number": "Exercise 4.5.2",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "orthogonality-of-degenerate-eigenstates",
   "prompt": "<p>Find $N$ that normalizes $\\psi$ if $\\psi = N(φ_1 − Sφ_2)$ where $φ_1$ and $φ_2$ are normalized wavefunctions and $S$ is their overlap integral.</p>\n$$ S= \\langle φ_1 | φ_2 \\rangle $$",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "source",
    "solution": "<p>Remember that to normalize an arbitrary wavefunction, we find a constant $N$ such that $\\langle \\psi | \\psi \\rangle = 1$. This equates to the following procedure:</p>\n$$ \\begin{aligned} \\langle\\psi | \\psi\\rangle =\\left\\langle N\\left(φ_{1} - Sφ_{2}\\right) | N\\left(φ_{1} - Sφ_{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle &= 1 \\\\[4pt] N^2\\left\\langle \\left(φ_{1} - Sφ_{2}\\right) | \\left(φ_{1}-Sφ_{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle &=1 \\\\[4pt] N^2 \\left[ \\overset{\\to\\,1}{\\cancel{\\langle φ_{1}|φ_{1}\\rangle}} - S \\overset{\\to\\,S}{\\cancel{\\langle φ_{2}|φ_{1}\\rangle}} - S \\overset{\\to\\,S}{\\cancel{\\langle φ_{1}|φ_{2}\\rangle}} + S^2 \\overset{\\to\\,1}{\\cancel{\\langle φ_{2}| φ_{2}\\rangle}} \\right] &= 1 \\\\[4pt] N^2(1 - S^2 \\cancel{-S^2} + \\cancel{S^2})&=1 \\\\[4pt] N^2(1-S^2) &= 1 \\end{aligned} $$\n<p>therefore</p>\n$$ N = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-S^2}} $$"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "This can be repeated an infinite number of times",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "hermitian-operators",
   "prompt": "<p>The particle-in-a-box wavefunctions $\\psi_1 = \\sqrt{2/L}\\,\\sin(\\pi x/L)$ and $\\psi_3 = \\sqrt{2/L}\\,\\sin(3\\pi x/L)$ are both even about the center of the box, so the symmetry argument of Example 4.5.1 does not show that they are orthogonal. Evaluate $\\int_0^L \\psi_1\\psi_3\\,dx$ to decide whether they are.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Use $\\sin A\\sin B = \\tfrac{1}{2}[\\cos(A-B) - \\cos(A+B)]$.",
    "solution": "<p>With $\\sin A\\sin B = \\tfrac{1}{2}[\\cos(A-B) - \\cos(A+B)]$,</p>$$ \\int_0^L \\psi_1\\psi_3\\,dx = \\dfrac{2}{L}\\int_0^L \\sin\\dfrac{\\pi x}{L}\\sin\\dfrac{3\\pi x}{L}\\,dx = \\dfrac{1}{L}\\int_0^L \\left[\\cos\\dfrac{2\\pi x}{L} - \\cos\\dfrac{4\\pi x}{L}\\right]dx $$$$ = \\dfrac{1}{L}\\left[\\dfrac{L}{2\\pi}\\sin\\dfrac{2\\pi x}{L} - \\dfrac{L}{4\\pi}\\sin\\dfrac{4\\pi x}{L}\\right]_0^L = 0 $$<p>since $\\sin 2\\pi = \\sin 4\\pi = \\sin 0 = 0$. The two functions are orthogonal, as the Orthogonality Theorem requires: they are eigenfunctions of the Hamiltonian with the different energies $E_1$ and $9E_1$. Their product is even about $L/2$, so the two halves of the integral do not cancel each other; each half is zero by itself.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
